+### Unused border corrections retain sample registers
+
+- **Symptom:** material pipelines spill registers even when every sampler uses a native Metal border colour.
+- **Cause:** the uniform mask branch in `dm_bs` keeps the twin sample's operands live even when no draw needs the correction.
+- **Fix:** every shader that corrects a sample exposes `dm_border_off` (`DMSH_BORDER_FC`, 216). An undefined constant preserves the corrections. `border_need` checks registered sampler parameters before lazy Metal sampler creation; `pso_key.border` distinguishes draws that need corrections. Vertex, fragment, task and mesh stages, including the vertex-pulling replacement, and compute dispatches (`cs_fn`) specialize the constant to true only when none of their corrected samples needs a twin. `bd_off` copies previous function constants and caches by the descriptor the function was noted with (`fn4_of`). Metal hands out one `MTLFunction` per function content, device-wide (`newFunctionWithName:` on the same library, or on another library loaded from the same data, returns the same object on macOS 27.2), while the host keys a specialization by its function object (`fn4_note`, `fn_dev`, `pbase_key`): on the Metal 4 path the border-off specialization is therefore a proxy (`FnVariant`) with its own descriptor and `fn_dev` record, which forwards every other message to the base function; noting the new constants on the shared object would have taken the corrections from every draw of that shader. Mesh pipelines materialize the specialization through `fn_dev`, with generated geometry entries recording their descriptors through `geo_mesh_entry`. Pair prebuilds use the same border-off specialization (`pre_fn`), and so does the offline pipeline archive (`pso_offline.py` `HOST_CONSTANTS`: metal-tt specializes a `<key>_b` or `<key>_bp` function, and the host finds the base by its specialized descriptor), so the pairs stay in the app's archive. Older serialized rows read as border-off and live draws needing corrections have a different key. `DM_NVN_BORDER_KEEP=1` keeps the corrections in every pipeline (diagnostic; `ab` alternates it with `DM_AB`). A compute shader with constants is loaded like the other stages (`fn_spec`): `cps_new` looks its pipeline up by the specialized descriptor, in the bundle and the device's archives, builds it through the MTLDevice from `fn_dev`'s specialization on a miss and captures it; compute pipelines are keyed by shader and variant (`cps_key`), and the prewarm builds the border-off one. Geometry kernels keep the corrections (`fn_dev`). Sampler arrays, gathers and comparisons retain the existing limitations described above.
+- **Found on:** [Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md#unused-border-corrections): its 1080p material passes spilled 300-400 bytes a thread; in-process A/B on a MacBook Air M4, 1.8 ms of a 26.5 ms frame (1.5 ms in the render stages, the rest in its kernels, NDSR's network among them).
+- **Check:** `t_pso_skey` covers old row lengths; `t_border --cpu` covers first use before sampler creation, per-stage bindings and sampler re-registration. The GPU cases in `t_border` check preservation of existing constants, the original function and prewarm specialization, each on a kernel of its own content, alongside the existing filtered-border samples. The rewritten kernel of `test_dmsh_border.py` runs on the GPU through `cps_new`, with the corrections and without them (`cs_fn`). `test_pso_offline.py` checks the constant names and the specialized bases and compute pipelines.
 
